00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
labour's shadow business secretary  johnson reynolds is with us now  obviously we want to talk about cost of  living and the impact on so many of our  viewers before that though what's putin  up to  well he's losing and i think we've got  to stand firm in the face of the threats  and aggression frankly i feel sorry for  any  russian conscripted forces sent to  ukraine what we know so far is  well-trained forces well-motivated  forces with the right equipment  are the people with the advantage and  the success so far so we can never  underestimate putin but he's losing and  we've got to stick to what we're doing  um and um his  threat  about nuclear weapons says he's um he's  not bluffing how seriously should we  take that  we can't underestimate him as i say but  i think fundamentally that comes from a  position of weakness you know  even people sympathetic to ukraine  thought it would be very difficult for  them to hold out for maybe a week  against russian forces and what we've  seen has completely contradicted that  and that position of weakness leads to  what putin is saying today now i think  we have to be very firm in saying  there'll be severe consequences not just  for that threat but for any kind of use  of chemical weapons which we've seen  from russia in the past but  fundamentally we should take heart from  the fact he is losing the russians are  losing ukraine is going to liberate  itself we continue to support them  that's what we must do and the referenda  there is four separate referenda that  he's planning in regions um of ukraine  in order to annex those regions well i  think  the claim that russia made and has made  consistently towards ukraine that these  are people who wish to be part of russia  or be sympathetic or break away from any  kiev  orientated government is completely  contradicted by the treatment of  ukrainians by russian forces during this  conflict so i'm afraid i see no  legitimacy in the claims that he is  making in regards to the eastern side of  ukraine  what's going on in ukraine is very much  of course impacting on the living  standards of people here in the united  kingdom we have a mini budget it's not  being called that but that's what it is  on friday what are you hoping for from  it  well i'm hoping for something very  different to what has been trailed over  the last few weeks i want to see a real  plan for growth i accept all of us do on  the labor side the government's claim  and point that their their running of  the economy for the last 12 years has  been poor and it hasn't grown but what  i've heard from the government so far  doesn't fill me with any confidence they  understand what really needs to happen  so getting rid of the cap on bankers  bonuses or ripping up people's  employment rights that is not the way to  grow this economy for the benefit of  everyone it's not the way to grow it at  all and i'm afraid that joe biden is  right and liz trust is wrong in how they  see the economy and what needs to be  done so i would like a long-term  industrial policy i'd like reform of  business rights i would like to see that  the brexit deal improved all of the  things that would really benefit  economic growth and households and wages  in this country now i'm afraid i think  we won't get any of that and it'll be a  huge mistake but stimulating the economy  starts with  businesses getting back on their feet  and that would include i suppose bankers  who because they'll get no cap on their  bonus they'll want to work here more and  they'll stimulate the economy well i  don't agree with the premise of  trickle-down economics i don't think  well i think actually the government's  argument is is to do with the the way us  investment banks might transfer more  staff to london because they're  enumerated in different ways let's  remember i mean the current cap is is  200 percent of your basic salary 300  percent i think of shareholders agree it  was designed for not for punitive  measures but for economic stability  because of the risks that were taken  leading up to the financial crisis and  the idea that if you incentivize the  bonus based on certain behavior that  could make it riskier and actually  that's a threat to economic stability so  i'd like to see a compelling case from  the government but the idea that in  itself lifting the cap on bankers  bonuses will grow the economy when it  will not have any impact on 99.9 of of  people in this country and crucially  will undermine the big fight which is  against inflation because how will the  government say to public sector workers  or people in the private sector we don't  want to get into a 1970s wage spiral  limit your own claims oh but by the way  we're lifting the cap on bankers bonuses  i find that incoherent yeah just looking  at um the  cap  on energy bills though and that would um  that would suggest that you know a lot  of people will benefit from that and it  will also start to control inflation yes  well we led the way over the summer  saying there must be a cap and a  protection for households and businesses  from these rising energy prices  obviously i do expect the government  from what we've heard to be in the same  place when the details come out today do  you agree with how they want to pay for  it no i think that's the big difference  the two big differences are first of all  how it should be paid for and is there a  plan alongside that to reduce our  dependence on fossil fuels and our  exposure to those prices on on paying  for it we would raise a substantial  proportion of the money from a windfall  tax because for us business profits  enough though well actually if you  extend electricity generators you're  getting towards figures above 20 billion  pounds that's not an insignificant sum  of money fundamentally okay profits  should come from businesses innovating  disrupting markets providing services  not from an artificial spike in prices  from a conflict from russian aggression  in ukraine and a lot of business people  accept that case and i think the fact  the government wants to put all of this  cost onto households and says you can  increase spending cut taxes and somehow  that will all be fine because we're  going to rip up people's employment  rights and these supply side reforms  will make the difference i'm afraid that  is not a that's not a convincing case i  want to come back to the windfall tax  that you were just talking about who who  would you tax so the tax that we put  forward is the existing tax that the  government themselves are actually  sticking to partly we would take away  the loophole for fossil fuel investments  because that's not something we should  be incentivizing in an energy crisis and  if that was extended to electricity  generators as ed miliband has said that  the estimate for that would be somewhere  between 10 and 14 billion pounds  additionally that would be raised so  there is substantial money that could  ease the burden on households if the  government chose to do so but it's a  question of whose side they're on  another thing that might help is  fracking where are you with that well  we're completely opposed to fracking  unless the government's safety advice  has changed and i understand it isn't  going to change i can't see how any  community will go for that crucial issue  here is fracking will not even if it was  very successful and accepted by the  public reduce the price of natural gas  because it would be sold at the market  rate for the european commodity market  and therefore you'd have a bit more  liquidity in the market if you had more  supply in the uk but it can't reduce the  price and fundamentally what it will do  by by undermining the government's  claims on its ambitions on net zero and  the objectives which are legally set in  this country it will increase the risk  premium and the cost of capital for the  investments we really need in nuclear  renewables energy efficiency and these  mixed messages that this government  always sends to business are one of the  reasons why business investment is so  poor to the conservatives and one of the  reasons why economic growth has also  been so poor and until they understand  that these gimmicks these half measures  they put forward will simply not do the  job and they'll fail on their own terms  and reynolds always a pleasure thank you  very much thank you

